Simple & Real-World Amazon RDS with Read Replicas

one of the most used AWS services in production — Amazon RDS 
Not only I launched a database, but I also configured Multi-AZ for high availability and a Read Replica for performance scaling 


🔍 What is Amazon RDS?

Amazon RDS is a managed database service where AWS takes care of:
🔹 Setup
🔹 Backups
🔹 Updates
🔹 Replication
🔹 Monitoring

It supports: MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server, MariaDB, and Aurora.

My Hands-On Steps

🔹 Step 1: Selected MySQL 8.0 as the database engine

Great for open-source apps and easy to connect from EC2.

🔹 Step 2: Created Primary RDS Instance

– Instance type: db.t3.medium
– Storage: 20 GB gp2
– Multi-AZ: Enabled 
– Encryption: Enabled with KMS
– Public access: Disabled
– Security Group: Allowed port 3306 only for EC2 app servers

🔹 Step 3: Monitoring Setup

– Enabled enhanced monitoring (1-min interval)
– Enabled slow query & general logs
– Integrated with CloudWatch Logs

🔹 Step 4: Created Read Replica

– Source: Primary RDS
– Name: replica-db-readonly
– Same region
– Enabled auto-backups
– IAM Authentication: On
– Used for reporting/dashboard queries

Why Use Read Replica?
To reduce load on the main DB and handle read-heavy traffic like analytics or reports.


Final Checks & Tests I Did

🔹 Connected app to main RDS
🔹 Ran SELECT queries from Read Replica
🔹 Rebooted RDS to test failover
🔹 Checked replication delay
🔹 Promoted Read Replica to standalone DB (Disaster Recovery test)

What I Learned Today:

🔹 Use Multi-AZ for high availability
🔹 Use Read Replicas to scale reads without affecting production
🔹 Always use private subnets for database
🔹 Monitor replication lag
🔹 Practice backup, restore & replica promotion regularly
